@100  = ~Perhaps it should be me who broaches this subject. It has been some time since we last saw each other (though it could have been longer). I am clearly an even better mage than I was then. Since we parted ways, I have been researching arcane theories beyond your meager comprehension and enhancing my talents to new heights. A far better usage of time than whatever you were doing (of that, I am certain).~
@101  = ~I was being tortured by an insane mage under Athkatla.~
@102  = ~I was having a great time in Baldur’s Gate, enjoying all the luxuries I deserved after defeating Sarevok, before I was kidnapped.~
@103  = ~…we defeated the Shining Crusade. You were there, or at least, until I was imprisoned.~
@104  = ~I spent my time training. I’m glad I did, but I wish I could’ve done more of that.~
@105  = ~I was also researching arcane theories. Likely beyond *your* comprehension.~
@106  = ~It’s really none of your business.~
@107  = ~Like I said, I had a far better use of time. You would do well to follow my example. But come, I have learned of your situation through my contacts in the Shadow Thieves, and I know you need a substantial amount of money to rescue Imoen. How and why you let her be taken by the Cowled Wizards is a mystery to me. But luckily for you, I always protect my subordinates.~
@108  = ~I’m not your subordinate.~
@109  = ~You are MY subordinate.~
@110  = ~Gee, thanks.~
@111  = ~Everyone who travels with a Red Wizard of Thay is subservient to the Wizard. It is only the natural order of things (after all, we are intelligent and powerful–moreso than our lessers, and thus more deserving to be in positions of authority). Now, let us be off.~
@112  = ~I am not! Everyone who travels with a Red Wizard of Thay is subservient to the Wizard. It is only the natural order of things (after all, we are intelligent and powerful–moreso than our lessers, and thus more deserving to be in positions of authority). Now, let us be off.~
@113  = ~You’re very welcome. Now, let us be off.~
@114  = ~Hmph. I have been in Athkatla for some time now, and it hasn’t gotten any better. I have traveled to many different places, and this is, by far, the worst (even Nashkel was more appealing). In addition, the present company leaves much to be desired. Though I suppose you have made small improvements since we last traveled together.~
@115  = ~I’ve made more than small improvements.~
@116  = ~I am very close to kicking you out of the group.~
@117  = ~Disagree, I’m growing fond of the place.~
@118  = ~I agree. Athkatla is pretty awful.~
@119  = ~And yet you still let Imoen and Irenicus get taken by those cowled charlatans. At least you managed to defeat Mae’Var and have not been killed by anything else yet (though who knows how long that will last).~
@120  = ~I didn’t just “let” Imoen get taken away!~
@121  = ~And I’m going to get Imoen back, no matter the cost.~
@122  = ~And I’m going to get Imoen back. You’ll see.~
@123  = ~No, you were just too pathetic to stop it. You may have done your best, but you failed anyway. If your best isn’t good enough, then the improvements you have made must be very small indeed (else you would be more successful). Your meager conversational skills have started to bore me, so let us continue onwards.~
@124  = ~“No matter the cost” is a bold statement. Whether you have the stomach for such ruthlessness remains to be seen (and hopefully we’ll all still be alive to witness it). This conversation has started to bore me, so let us continue onwards.~
@125  = ~Hmph. That remains to be seen (and hopefully we’ll all still be alive to witness it). Your meager conversational skills have started to bore me, so let us continue onwards.~
@126  = ~You need my powerful magics. Your bluffing has no effect on me. Your conversational skills are lacking, and have begun to bore me. Let us continue onwards.~
@127  = ~Of all the things to be illegal. Magic. Clearly the simian population is both jealous and afraid of magical talents (which I possess in abundance).~
@128  = ~Yes, Edwin, you are very talented.~
@129  = ~Don't flatter yourself.~
@130  = ~Of all the things to be illegal. Magic. Clearly the simian population is both jealous and afraid of magical talents (which we possess in abundance).~
@131  = ~…Did you just say that I have abundant magical talent?~
@132  = ~Do not take it as a compliment. You have merely continued to survive despite anyone’s expectations. Though you may have magical talent (not equal to my own, mind you), talent is not enough. To fully realize your potential, you also need an outstanding intellect and an excellent education, which you are lacking.~
@133  = ~You sure know how to ruin a moment, don’t you?~
@134  = ~Gorion was a great wizard, and he and Tethtoril gave me an excellent education. I won’t just let you insult them. Keep it up and I’ll give your spellbook to the nearest child with crayons.~
@135  = ~I am glad that you realize this. Perhaps you are not as unobservant as I had thought. But we have wasted too much time with idle prattle already; let us be off.~
@136  = ~It is not flattery if it is the truth. Then, it is simply an observation. Your meager conversational skills have started to bore me, so let us continue onwards.~
@137  = ~If speaking the truth ruins a moment, then you are a fool, and I am wasted on you. Your meager conversational skills have started to bore me, so let us continue onwards.~
@138  = ~No education can compare to the schools of Thay. No matter your education in Candlekeep, it was inadequate compared to the proper Thayvian education that I myself received. Perhaps one day, you will be deserving of the details (though not today). But we have wasted too much time with idle prattle already; let us be off.~
@139  = ~Your taste is even worse than I thought then. You are a buffoon, and you are lucky to be traveling with me. Both your taste and conversational skills are lacking, and have begun to bore me. Let us continue onwards.~
@140  = ~Hmph. You have terrible taste in companions (really, you should be letting me decide who comes with us).~
@141  = ~Terrible taste, yet I let you tag along.~
@142  = ~My bad taste is the only reason why you’re here.~
@143  = ~I have great taste, actually. As proven by your presence.~
@144  = ~(Snap and go on a lengthy rant, with plenty of insults for good measure).~
@145  = ~Stick to the spells, Edwin.~
@146  = ~Even the most incompetent fool can be successful once or twice. My presence proves that you are not completely a lost cause (and whether that changes remains to be seen). Tell me, are there companions of yours here just to distract our enemies on the battlefield, or do you feel useless sentimentality towards them?~
@147  = ~Sentimentality isn’t useless.~
@148  = ~They’re here because they’re convenient. As soon as they stop being so, they are gone.~
@149  = ~Pfah! Do you accept every insult thrown your way? My presence proves that you are not completely a lost cause (and whether that changes remains to be seen–though I have my doubts). Tell me, are there companions of yours here just to distract our enemies on the battlefield, or do you feel useless sentimentality towards them?~
@150  = ~Hmph. And yet you let the rest of these simians travel with us. Tell me, are there companions of yours here just to distract our enemies on the battlefield, or do you feel useless sentimentality towards them?~
@151  = ~You insults need work, but the quality is more than I would’ve expected from a simian such as yourself. Perhaps there is hope for you yet. Tell me, are there companions of yours here just to distract our enemies on the battlefield, or do you feel useless sentimentality towards them?~
@152  = ~Sentimentality is for fools waiting to be used and betrayed. Did your foolish Harper of a foster father teach you this nonsense?~
@153  = ~It’s not nonsense, and Gorion wasn’t foolish! Friendship and love make life more meaningful.~
@154  = ~You’re more prickly than usual. Someone not get enough beauty sleep last night?~
@155  = ~Edwin, shut up. This conversation is over.~
@156  = ~Does it? Look at you, running around Athkatla like a common mercenary, pinching coins like a beggar for the mere chance of rescuing Imoen (and dragging me along with you).~
@157  = ~I’m after Irenicus, not Imoen.~
@158  = ~I’m not dragging you along. You’re free to leave whenever you want. Unless it’s sentimentality keeping you here?~
@159  = ~Hmph. Look at you, running around Athkatla like a common mercenary, pinching coins like a beggar to rescue Imoen (and dragging me along with you).~
@160  = ~Hmph. You say that friendship makes life more meaningful, yet you leave those who would call you a friend, languishing in captivity (the hypocrisy is sickening). But clearly, my wisdom is wasted on you. This conversation is over.~
@161  = ~Hmph. I thought as much. There is no sentimentality between us, nor will there ever be (and I am glad you realize this).~
@162  = ~ Pfah! My reasons are much more practical. There is no sentimentality between us, nor will there ever be (and you would be a fool to think otherwise). Clearly, my wisdom is wasted on you. This conversation is over.~
@163  = ~ Clearly, my wisdom is wasted on you.~
@164  = ~Hmph. Out with it already. I know you want to comment on my situation like the rest of these simians.~
@165  = ~This is why you don’t mess with magical items beyond your power.~
@166  = ~I wasn’t going to comment. I know what it’s like to be seen as a gender that you’re not.~
@167  = ~We’ll resolve this situation somehow.~
@168  = ~This is different. I am Edwin Odesseiron, and it wasn’t supposed to be like this! I was supposed to bring my power to new heights, not.. not have THIS happen!~
@169  = ~Ha! You’re right, maybe you are stuck like this. You’d deserve it for messing with the Nether Scroll.~
@170  = ~I don’t know, but we’ll find a way. Somehow.~
@171  = ~And if we can’t? If I’m stuck in this wretched form forever?~
@172  = ~That would be unfortunate. But you’re still the same Edwin to me, you know, regardless of what form you take.~
@173  = ~Then you’d deserve it. It'd be a fitting punishment for your arrogance.~
@174  = ~Hmph. Let us find something to do, some sort of distraction from being in this form!~
@175  = ~There is no magical item beyond the power of Edwin Odesseiron, Red Wizard of Thay! This was NOT SUPPOSED TO HAPPEN. I do not need a lecture from the likes of you.~
@176  = ~You could have sold me out to Degardiagananan but you did not. That was a wise decision, and I am almost impressed.~
@177  = ~Are you trying to thank me?~
@178  = ~We’re friends, of course I wouldn’t sell you out.~
@179  = ~I am starting to regret my decision already.~
@180  = ~Of course not! A Red Wizard thanks no one (lest we be mistaken for being indebted)!~
@181  = ~It sounds to me like you’re trying to thank me. It’s not going to kill you, Edwin.~
@182  = ~You aren’t indebted; it’s what anyone would have done.~
@183  = ~If you say so.~
@184  = ~You are a presumptuous buffoon, and this conversation is a waste of time. Now, come. We have many an inane task ahead of us (and little time to waste on such prattle).~
@185  = ~You’d be surprised <CHARNAME> (and your naivety sickens me). This conversation is a waste of time. Now, come. We have many an inane task ahead of us (and little time to waste on such prattle).~
@186  = ~And I do. This conversation is a waste of time. Now, come. We have many an inane task ahead of us (and little time to waste on such prattle).~
@187  = ~Friends? Friendship is for sentimental fools, <CHARNAME> (and for those who are too weak to stand on their own). Have I given you any reason to think we are “friends”?~
@188  = ~No, I suppose not, especially with how often you insult me.~
@189  = ~You’ve stuck around this long, haven’t you?~
@190  = ~We’re friends, and there’s not much you can do about it.~
@191  = ~Insult Gorion again and I’ll set your spellbook on fire.~
@192  = ~The insults are deserved (of that, I assure you). Let me promise you that I am here for my own reasons and interests. here is no sentimentality between us, no matter how much you may wish it! Just because I let you in my company and just because we rely on each other on the battlefield does not mean we are *friends*. Now, come. We have many an inane task ahead of us (and little time to waste on such prattle).~
@193  = ~Pfah! For my own reasons and interests, of course. There is no sentimentality between us, no matter how much you may wish it! Just because I let you in my company and just because we rely on each other on the battlefield does not mean we are *friends*. Now, come. We have many an inane task ahead of us (and little time to waste on such prattle).~
@194  = ~Pfah! I have heard that friendship is supposed to be a two-way street and that it is not meant to be forced. Now, come. We have many an inane task ahead of us (and little time to waste on such prattle). ~
@195  = ~You act as if I would allow you to do such a thing (and as if my spellbook isn’t magically fireproof). There is no sentimentality between us, no matter how much you may wish it! Just because I let you in my company and just because we rely on each other on the battlefield does not mean we are *friends*. Now, come. We have many an inane task ahead of us (and little time to waste on such prattle).~
@196  = ~Pfah! As if you could survive without my magics! You need me (even if you are too stubborn to admit it!). This conversation bores me. Now, come. We have many an inane task ahead of us (and little time to waste on such prattle).~
@197  = ~So, our chase nears its end. Imoen and Irenicus are now within our grasp. All we have to do is storm the asylum created specifically to hold (and likely perform gruesome experiments upon) spellcasters such as myself.~
@198  = ~It doesn’t sound very appealing, does it? Still, we have no other choice.~
@199  = ~Getting nervous?~
@200  = ~You’re lucky you aren’t in there yourself.~
@201  = ~We’ll be fine, I’m sure. I won’t let anything happen to you.~
@202  = ~So, our chase nears its end. Imoen and Irenicus are now within our grasp. All we have to do is storm the asylum created specifically to hold (and likely perform gruesome experiments upon) spellcasters such as ourselves.~
@203  = ~No, I suppose not. Those incompetent cowled charlatans will be no match for us (or at least, no match for me).~
@204  = ~Wish I had your confidence.~
@205  = ~Don’t get too arrogant and careless.~
@206  = ~That’s right! The two of us can do it.~
@207  = ~Pfah!As if Edwin Odessieron would be nervous.~
@208  = ~Pfah!As if Edwin Odessieron would let those cowled charlatans whisk me away.~
@209  = ~Pfah! As if there was ever any chance that anything would happen to Edwin Odesseiron.~
@210  = ~If you had received the excellent Thayvian education and were a Red Wizard like myself, then you would have confidence also. As it stands, you are right to be nervous, but Edwin Odesseiron is with you. Together, we shall crush the Cowled Wizards beneath our heels and rescue Imoen, who you so carelessly let get captured (you are lucky I am here to help fix your mistakes).~
@211  = ~I didn’t make a mistake!~
@212  = ~..Thank you.~
@213  = ~It is not arrogant and I am never careless (do NOT mention the Nether Scroll). If you had received the excellent Thayvian education and were a Red Wizard like myself, then you would have confidence also. As it stands, you are right to be nervous, but Edwin Odesseiron is with you. Together, we shall crush the Cowled Wizards beneath our heels and rescue Imoen, who you so carelessly let get captured (you are lucky I am here to help fix your mistakes).~
@214  = ~Hmph. I am glad you realize this. Try to stay out of the way of my spells. I do not expect you to be able to keep up, as you are not me and are lacking the excellent Thayvian education which I received. Edwin Odesseiron is with you. Together, we shall crush the Cowled Wizards beneath our heels and rescue Imoen, who you so carelessly let get captured (you are lucky I am here to help fix your mistakes).~
@215  = ~...Thank you.~
@216  = ~No? You let Imoen get taken on purpose (no, I thought not). We cannot all be myself, sometimes, lesser beings make mistakes (do NOT mention the Nether Scroll). Now, enough of this idle chatter.~
@217  = ~Hmph. Edwin Odesseiron always takes care of his subordinates. Now, enough of this idle chatter.~
@218  = ~Hmph. Something is bothering you. I can tell. Out with it.~
@219  = ~Gee, Edwin, I don’t know what could POSSIBLY be wrong.~
@220  = ~Well, let’s see. I DID just get my soul stolen so that’s a bummer.~
@221  = ~Are you concerned about me?~
@222  = ~I’m fine.~
@223  = ~No, you are not fine. You have a sullen silence around you. It screams I-am-not-fine, and it is distracting me from my spellbook.~
@224  = ~You aren’t going to drop this, are you?~
@225  = ~No. Now, out with it. I could be doing something more useful with my time.~
@226  = ~So you lost your soul. Yet you are somehow still cognizant and not a drooling catatonic lump. You have overcome plenty, and with my considerable talents, we will get your soul back (of that I am certain).~
@227  = ~I wish I had your optimism.~
@228  = ~You’re right. We’ll get through this.~
@229  = ~Pfah! I am not concerned! Although you lost your soul, you are somehow still cognizant and not a drooling catatonic lump. You have overcome plenty, and with my considerable talents, we will get your soul back (of that I am certain).~
@230  = ~It is not optimism. Optimism is for naive fools who rely on hope. Confidence, on the other hand, is only natural to those who are competent enough to succeed (such as myself). Now, stop your sulking and leave me to my spellbook.~
@231  = ~Of course I am right. I do not need you to tell me as such, but I am glad you realize this fact. You have overcome plenty, and with my considerable talents, we will get your soul back (of that I am certain).  Now, stop your sulking and leave me to my spellbook.~
@232  = ~See, <CHARNAME>? This wretch was no match for our combined power. Soon, we shall confront Irenicus, and he too shall fall before our might (and I’ll make sure of it).~
@233  = ~Yes, and this entire situation will finally be over.~
@234  = ~You’re optimistic.~
@235  = ~I’m glad you’re here.~
@236  = ~And all your mistakes will be fixed. It will be as if you were never captured by Irenicus in the first place.~
@237  = ~I don’t think I’ll ever go back to who I used to be.~
@238  = ~You are much more competent and capable than you were in Baldur’s Gate. Why regret the power you have gained? (Only a fool would)!~
@239  = ~Edwin, are you… complimenting me?~
@240  = ~I don’t regret the growth; I regret how it happened.~
@241  = ~It is not optimism! It is confidence, <CHARNAME>. Perhaps if you had an excellent Thayvian education like myself, rather than that Harper nonsense, you would be confident also. One thing that my excellent Thayvian education has taught me is that growth is not easy. You must defeat all your rivals and those that oppose you to make it to the top. Bodhi has fallen before our might, and soon, Irenicus will be beneath our heel as well. But come. You may be more competent, but you are still not deserving of more secrets of my excellent Thayvian education (perhaps later).~
@242  = ~Hmph. Yes, of course you are. I am a powerful and accomplished Red Wizard, and anyone would be thrilled to have me around (or else their taste would be truly wretched indeed). I will tell you something I learned in Thay. One thing that my excellent Thayvian education has taught me is that growth is not easy. You must defeat all your rivals and those that oppose you to make it to the top. Bodhi has fallen before our might, and soon, Irenicus will be beneath our heel as well. But come. You may be more competent, but you are still not deserving of more secrets of my excellent Thayvian education (perhaps later).~
@243  = ~No! I am not complimenting you. I am merely stating facts (do not let it get to your head). Bodhi has fallen before our might, and soon, Irenicus will be beneath our heel as well. But come. You may be more competent, but you are still not deserving of more secrets of my excellent Thayvian education (perhaps later).~
@244  = ~One thing that my excellent Thayvian education has taught me is that growth is not easy. You must defeat all your rivals and those that oppose you to make it to the top. Bodhi has fallen before our might, and soon, Irenicus will be beneath our heel as well. But come. You may be more competent, but you are still not deserving of more secrets of my excellent Thayvian education (perhaps later).~
@245  = ~So, I think… We shall confront Irenicus very soon.~
@246  = ~This is my fight, not yours.~
@247  = ~You aren’t scared, are you?~
@248  = ~Let’s hope we don’t die.~
@249  = ~Pfah! Do you remember what I said in Athkatla, shortly after we joined forces? I said that I always protect my subordinates.~
@250  = ~And--~
@251  = ~I--~
@252  = ~But--~
@253  = ~Let me say my piece (and without interruption). I… I do not consider you a subordinate anymore, nor an inferior in any way, even though you never had an adequate arcane Thayvian education like myself.~
@254  = ~Thanks, I--~
@255  = ~Edwin--~
@256  = ~Let me finish. One, or both of us, could very well be killed by Irenicus (unfortunate, but true). I do not want there to be things left unsaid between us. Despite all the things that have happened between us, both good and bad, I… I need to tell you, that… That I count you as a true friend (and that is NOT something that I say often!). And as such, I will stand by you against Irenicus, even though it might cost me my life.~
@257  = ~You may not feel the same; I know that. But I wanted you to know.~
@258  = ~If you would just shut up for once and let me talk! I… count you as a true friend, too. (NOTE: Edwin's Alignment will change to Lawful Neutral).~
@259  = ~If you would just shut up for once and let me talk! I… count you as a true friend, too. (NOTE: Edwin's Alignment will stay Lawful Evil)~
@260  = ~Edwin, I’m sorry, but I only consider you a colleague.~
@261  = ~I am glad to hear it. Perhaps… Perhaps if we both get out of this alive, we can figure out our futures from there.~
@262  = ~I… see. Well, best prepare for whatever we face ahead.~
